  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracts in Hawaii totaled $2.3B in obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government awarded 21,010 contracts in the state, with an average award size of $109K. Obligations fell 58.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show higher recent totals: FY21 was $2.8B, FY22 was $3.5B, FY23 was $4.4B, FY24 was $6.7B, FY25 was $5.5B, and FY26 is $2.3B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $1.8B so far in FY2026. The General Services Administration obligated $284M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $115M in the same period. Other large agencies with FY2026 obligations include the Department of Veterans Affairs with $45.3M, the Department of the Interior with $35.8M, and the Department of Labor with $16.9M. Construction and engineering work leads industry activity in FY2026 to date, with Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $602M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $391M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Hawaii?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $1.8B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration and the Department of Homeland Security follow with $284M and $115M respectively. Buying is concentrated in a few agencies while award counts remain high at 21,010, which keeps the average award size at $109K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What industries capture the most federal dollars in Hawaii?",
        "body": "Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $602M in FY2026 to date. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows with $391M, and Engineering Services is next with $284M. Computer Systems Design Services and Facilities Support Services also appear among the top industries with $131M and $103M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much federal contract money goes to Hawaii?",
      "a": "Hawaii received $2.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state had 21,010 awards with an average award size of $109K and obligations down 58.5% year over year. Historical annual obligations were $2.8B in FY21, $3.5B in FY22, $4.4B in FY23, $6.7B in FY24, and $5.5B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies award the most contracts in Hawaii?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense awards the most contracts in Hawaii, obligating $1.8B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $284M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $115M. Other significant buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs with $45.3M and the Department of the Interior with $35.8M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are the largest federal contractors in Hawaii?",
      "a": "DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV is the largest federal contractor in Hawaii with $354M in obligations in FY2026 to date. NAN INC is next with $272M and CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC follows with $97.3M. Other top vendors include LEIDOS, INC. with $83.9M and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP with $73.5M."
